  "summary": "A distinctive style of Islamic educational architecture found in Timbuktu, characterized by mud-brick construction and integrated educational facilities.",
  "description": "These madrasahs combine mosque structures with teaching spaces, featuring thick earth walls, interior courtyards, and wooden elements. They represent the integration of Islamic scholarship with West African building traditions, creating centers of learning that flourished during the Mali Empire.",
  "culturalContext": "Timbuktu's Djinguereber, Sankoré and Sidi Yahya mosques-madrasas (1325-1500s) — built under Mali's Mansa Musa and the Songhai era — made the city the \"city of 333 saints\" and a book-trade university town. Sahelian masons (guided by Andalusian poet-architect Abu Ishaq al-Sahili at Djinguereber) built banco mud-brick masses with projecting toron beams, stood each rainy season for community replastering (crépissage) as festival. The madrasahs' manuscript libraries — 700,000 documents — were smuggled safe from 2012 conflict, confirming their living status.",
